WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:04.775
The Eagle Nebula is a young open cluster of stars in the constellation of Serpens.

00:05.297 --> 00:15.769
Alternatively, it is catalogued as Messier 16, M16, or NGC 6611, and sometimes referred to as the Star Queen Nebula.

00:16.349 --> 00:21.873
This cluster was discovered by Jean-Philippe de Cheseaux in 1745–46.

00:22.349 --> 00:35.329
Both the "Eagle" and the "Star Queen" refer to visual impressions of the dark silhouette near the center of the nebula, an area made famous as the "Pillars of Creation" imaged by the Hubble Space Telescope.

00:35.911 --> 00:43.321
The nebula contains several active star-forming gas and dust regions, including the aforementioned Pillars of Creation.
